# 3. CRITICAL ROOT-CAUSE ANALYSIS OF THE EXISTING DESIGN

The following architectural assumptions must be challenged.

## Assumption A — Randomization creates originality

**Incorrect.**

A seeded PRNG that changes a few note positions, velocities, or pitches may produce different MIDI data while generating essentially the same music.

### Required correction

Implement hierarchical musical generation with motif development, phrase variation, harmonic context, rhythmic structure, arrangement progression, and timbral evolution.

## Assumption B — Four variations provide sufficient musical depth

**Incorrect.**

Four one-bar variations are inadequate for an advanced composition engine.

### Required correction

Support variable-duration phrases, motif families, independently evolving variations, and multi-section composition.

## Assumption C — Every underground techno track uses the same structural template

**Incorrect.**

A constant intro-build-breakdown-drop-outro structure produces predictable arrangements.

### Required correction

Develop a structural composition planner capable of selecting or inventing musically coherent macroforms based on the project.

## Assumption D — More random notes mean more creativity

**Incorrect.**

Unconstrained randomness often reduces groove, intent, and musical identity.

### Required correction

Use constrained novelty with coherent repetition, musical memory, controlled surprise, and phrase-level development.

## Assumption E — A changed MIDI pattern is enough

**Incorrect.**

High-level music generation must coordinate notes, envelopes, sound design, effects, routing, spatial movement, and arrangement energy.

### Required correction

Generate a complete production plan, not just MIDI.

## Assumption F — Gzip/XML export guarantees Ableton compatibility

**Incorrect.**

Native compatibility depends on valid version-specific device, automation, routing, and project structures.

### Required correction

Keep the existing Pako parser as a compatibility baseline while developing a loss-preserving, validated compiler with native fixture verification.

## Assumption G — Browser Web Audio equals native Android low-latency audio

**Incorrect.**

Android devices differ in WebView performance, output latency, memory limits, and audio focus behavior.

### Required correction

Introduce a native Android audio path when browser-based playback cannot meet validated performance targets.

## Assumption H — Estimated RMS can be labeled LUFS

**Incorrect.**

Replace approximate loudness labeling with a standards-based loudness measurement implementation, distinguishing LUFS from RMS and dBFS.

## Assumption I — A 25 ms JavaScript scheduler is automatically sample-accurate

**Incorrect.**

Main-thread timers can be delayed by garbage collection, UI activity, and lifecycle changes.

### Required correction

Separate lookahead event preparation from actual audio-clock scheduling and audio-thread DSP.

## Assumption J — Zero bugs and zero memory leaks can be promised

**Not a verifiable absolute guarantee.**

Implement aggressive defect prevention, leak detection, long-running stability tests, measurable performance budgets, and release-blocking severity policies instead.

The engineering objective is zero known critical defects in released supported workflows, with supporting test evidence.

